---
import DocsNav from "../../components/DocsNav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { docs, layers, localUrl, providers, sourceUrl } from "../../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Architecture — OrbitKV"
  description="How OrbitKV state ownership, Luminal compilation, and native Rust serving fit together."
  active="Architecture"
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Inside the architecture</p>
    <h1>
      State has one owner.
      <br />
      Execution has room to evolve.
    </h1>
    <p>
      OrbitKV connects attention semantics to physical state. Luminal connects
      mathematical graphs to CUDA implementations. Explicit contracts join the
      two.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="docs-layout">
    <DocsNav active="Architecture" />
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Three owned crates</h2>
      <p>
        The core can be used independently. The executor and engine compose it
        into a native inference process with one authority for KV and persistent
        model state.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Crate</th>
              <th scope="col">Responsibility</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {layers.map((layer) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <a href={sourceUrl(layer.path)}>
                    <code>{layer.name}</code>
                  </a>
                </td>
                <td>{layer.detail}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h2>From model semantics to execution</h2>
      <pre
        tabindex="0"
        aria-label="Compilation and execution pipeline"
      ><code>{`model configuration + mathematical operations
    │
    ├─ OrbitKV → state layout + lifetime plan
    │               │
    │          RuntimeSession
    │          pages · generations · fixed state
    │               │
    └─ Luminal ← state contracts + stable arenas
          │
          e-graph → legal candidates → CUDA profiling
          │
          selected program + execution artifacts
          │
          device completion → publication → safe reuse`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Model import normalizes configuration into reusable operations and state
        contracts. Implementations are admitted by semantics, dtype, shape,
        layout, and device capability. Checkpoint names do not choose kernels.
      </p>
      <h2>What Luminal searches</h2>
      <p>
        The inference-only fork retains four packages: the graph and e-graph
        core, NN helpers and semantic operations, CUDA Lite, and tracing. Core
        compilation produces a saturated search space; the CUDA runtime extracts
        legal programs, profiles candidates, and loads the selected schedule.
      </p>
      <p>
        External libraries contribute implementations alongside generated CUDA
        operations. Attention stays a semantic operation with an explicit KV
        view. The current executable view is paged NHD K/V, with causal or
        sliding visibility.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Provider</th>
              <th scope="col">Current integration</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {providers.map((provider) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{provider.name}</td>
                <td>
                  {provider.role}
                  <small>{provider.scope}</small>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Search compares the alternatives supplied by compiler rules; it does not
        guarantee a global optimum. CUDA Graph capture replays a sequence of
        launches. General megakernel generation and competition between multiple
        KV layouts remain on the{" "}
        <a href={sourceUrl("docs/joint-compilation.md")}>
          joint compilation roadmap
        </a>
        .
      </p>
      <h2>State outlives a kernel call</h2>
      <p>
        Full attention retains an append-only history. Sliding attention permits
        periodic placement and retirement beyond its window. Exact Chunked
        attention uses resettable epoch arenas. Recurrent and convolution layers
        carry fixed-size state with generation-checked ownership.
      </p>
      <p>
        A page becomes reusable after future attention can no longer reach it,
        submitted device work has completed, and the exact retired generation is
        acknowledged. Prefix sharing, copy-on-write, cancellation, and external
        transfers follow the same ownership rules.
      </p>
      <h2>Compile once, replay deliberately</h2>
      <p>
        Selected decode and prefill schedules can be saved with generated CUDA
        module images. Artifacts bind to model, state, arena, and compilation
        contracts; incompatible artifacts fail closed. External provider
        libraries use a separate cache, with sources prepared explicitly before
        model compilation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The engine retains materialized buckets under a graph-cache capacity,
        prepares selected representatives before readiness, and updates stable
        input allocations for execution. See{" "}
        <a href={sourceUrl("docs/module-artifacts.md")}>module artifacts</a>,{" "}
        <a href={sourceUrl("docs/graph-residency.md")}>graph residency</a>, and
        the <a href={localUrl("/evidence/")}>recorded qualification</a>.
      </p>
      <h2>Read further</h2>
      <div class="doc-grid">
        {docs.map((item) => (
          <div class="doc-card">
            <a href={sourceUrl(item.path)}>
              {item.name}
              <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
            </a>
            <p>{item.detail}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
